the history of colognes famous cathedral  began with a shrine adorned with 74  figures made from gilded silver it  contains what's said to be the relics of  the three kings the Magi  in the 12th century the Holy Roman  Emperor gave the relics which had long  been kept in Milan to the archbishop of  cologne the old cathedral was too small  to accommodate the pilgrims flocking to  see the relics so a new one had to be  built it was to be a monumental  structure visible from afar in the  Middle Ages Christians traveled great  distances to visit holy sites  work began in 1248 and the Gothic  cathedral was finally completed in 1880  for a time it was the world's tallest  structure Cologne Cathedral welcomes  everyone and there's no entry fee some  20,000 tourists and pilgrims pass  through its doors every day they come to  see the late 13th century Milan Madonna  or the triptych altarpiece of the patron  saints of cologne by Stefan Lana the  passing of the centuries is reflected in  the cathedrals stained glass windows too  they depict everything from biblical  stories to artists Gerhard Richter's  modern play of colors restoration and  conservation of the cathedral and it's  artworks is a never-ending job  built on the remains of the old  cathedral and the ancient Roman city  walls Cologne cathedrals volts hold even  more treasures they show how rich and  powerful the city's Archbishop's once  were the cityscape of colognes old town  is also dominated by its 12 Romanesque  churches  and the ruins of another church provided  the foundations for Columba the art  museum of the archdiocese of cologne  designed by swiss architect peter some  tour the city's newest art museum is  meant to be a place of reflection  there are works highlighting various  styles and errors from two millennia of  Western culture the exhibition space  invites visitors to take a closer look  the historical town hall is a testament  to colognes importance in the Middle  Ages when it was one of the busiest  trade centres in Europe in front of the  Town Hall visitors can see the remains  of the Praetorian the Roman governors  palace and what later became the Jewish  quarter cologne suffered extensive  damage during World War two only a small  part of the old town with its streets  and buildings dating from the Middle  Ages survived the museum Ludvik is well  known outside of cologne  it's named after Elena and Peter ludvík  who endowed their extensive collection  to the city thanks to the generosity of  other cologne art collectors as well the  museum features a collection of 20th  century and contemporary art  here visitors can see everything from  expressionist sculpture to pop art the  museum Ludwig's collection of pop art is  the largest one outside the US all the  icons of this art movement which enjoyed  its heyday in the 1960s are exhibited  here  a stroll along the Rhine is a must for  every cologne visitor the river is as  much a part of the city as the cathedral  it made cologne a trading City and it's  left its mark on the local character the  people of cologne are known for their  easygoing sociability  for nightlife head to the Belgian  quarter colognes young and hip meet at  bussola platts in bars like Hal McCann  Reuter  whether you're here for a day or a  lifetime cologne boasts enough variety  to make everyone feel at home
 

